 door and peeked out and the gunman was  still in the sanctuary looking for more  people to eliminate and saw him and he  came into the area and shot him and  meltus laid right by our feet and and  died did you see the gunman I did not  see him I saw his shadow could you hear  him he didn't say anything but you could  hear him coming in yes I guess when he  thought he got all of us he moved on to  another part of the building there was  an entrance into the kitchen at the  bottom of the steps  and he heard their voices so he knew  there were people in there and he went  in and shot them one of those people was  my brother I think the time that he  spent shooting them actually is what  gave us the time to escape the room and  to me they're their martyrs they gave us  the time so that we're still here Barry  called 911 Don the phone with him until  the SWAT team came to rescue us and we  were in the back of the patrol car for  probably an hour but we knew what was  going on because the radio was on in the  police cars and you listened to it all  on the radio yeah when did you realize  that your brother had been killed my  heart kept saying well maybe he managed  to escape although really there was  nowhere for him to go he and Dan were  we're trapped they were they were  sitting ducks there was no way they were  gonna survive you know I went to the  court hearing for the gunman I know he  pleaded not guilty when you hope he gets  the death penalty as prosecutors plan to  seek no because I think that's too easy  and I don't want him to have it easy I  want him to spend the rest of his  miserable life locked up because he has  no remorse and I think his only regret  is that he didn't get us all I'm  reminded listening to you that we all  grieve differently some people are  incapable of talking after something  like that  some people find it cathartic to talk  what is this interview for you talking  about it is in the sense cathartic for  me it makes me feel more human and more  alive I'm glad to be alive  she is remarkably composed yeah her  strength was profound  yeah and that she could that she could  lay out all the details in such a calm  fashion as kind of breathtaking and  she's she and she understands exactly  what happened I remember her saying how  do you do Endel down a life of more than  60 years you know in the one interview  the support in that community has been  one of those moments those seeing them  all rally around each other and not just  that community but the Jewish community  around the country and in the world at  least say those those people of  Pittsburgh particularly Squirrel Hill  are some of the most inspiring resilient  people I've ever met on the road you  know and you can just in that one woman  you see it all right there Americans  back to the Emanuel nine and people like  rallying around all of those victims all  right David big note thanks so much for  being with us this morning
 

